A||N||3||Elenor84||Removing Cam bearings without speacial tool?||||1||
Z||000000||Elenor84||12-12-2001||11:24 PM||||Hey guys...the block is completely done being striped down except for the cam bearings...I hear that I need a speacial tool...I obviously don't have it..is it possible without it?  Or should I just rent one?  Thx!<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||12-13-2001||12:01 AM||||Take it to the machine shop to get those removed and installed.  It's not only a very important step, but you'll save time and money having them do it with their specialised equipment rather than you trying to do it without a good knowledge of what you're doing.<P>My $.02<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Elenor84||12-13-2001||12:13 AM||||Thx Bryce!  Preciate it!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bikemanj||12-13-2001||02:06 AM||||If you are doing the rebuild yourself you will also want to have the shop put the new cam bearings in for you (After any machining). A cam bearing tool is the only way to do it right.||||reg||4
